p.src:
For a general observable $A(q,p,t)$, $\frac{dA}{dt}=\{A,H\}+\frac{\partial A}{\partial t}.$ Setting $A=H$ gives $\frac{dH}{dt}
 =\{H,H\}+\frac{\partial H}{\partial t}
 =\boxed{\frac{\partial H}{\partial t}},$ because every function has zero Poisson bracket with itself. Thus a time-independent Hamiltonian is conserved, while explicit time dependence measures the energy supplied to or removed from the chosen subsystem.

